{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And these are the kings.</b> Some say this passage was written by way of prophecy, and Yitzhaki said in his book that this passage was written in the days of Jehoshaphat, and he interpreted 'the generations' as he pleased. He called his name Yitzhaki accordingly — everyone who hears laughs at him, for he said that Hadar is Hadad the Edomite, and he said that Mehetabel is the sister of Tahpenes. Far be it, far be it, that the matter is as he spoke concerning the days of Jehoshaphat, and his book is fit to be burned. And why wonder at eight kings who reigned, that they are many? Behold, the kings of Israel are double in number, and these two kings are close to two kings of Israel, and also the kings of Judah are more numerous than the kings of Edom until the days of Moses. And the truth is that 'before' means before a king reigned over Israel, namely Moses king of Israel, and likewise it is written, 'And there was a king in Jeshurun' (Deuteronomy 33:5)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "Resolved 'יצחקי' as Yitzhaki (a specific interpreter Ibn Ezra polemicizes against); the pun on the name 'יצחק' / 'will laugh' is preserved literally. The phrase 'ושני אלה המלכים קרובים לשני מלכי ישראל' is textually difficult but translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:47:11.298041726Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}